      "content": "The atypical distribution pattern of your ceramic shards suggests post-depositional disturbance rather than a pristine original deposit. Causes for such disruption include later human activity, such as digging, construction, or even plowing. It's also possible that natural processes like burrowing animals, heavy rain, and even ancient floods could have disturbed the site's stratigraphy. Other factors may include the thin layer of ash (called a thermopile) that can sometimes separate strata, complicating the original context. It would be beneficial to evaluate the site for potential signs of later activity and to look for soot accumulation or trampling that might give you a better indication of the disturbance events. TAMPEREI—terrain analysis, modern engineering evaluation—techniques could be used to investigate the extent of such disturbances.",
